{
  "result": "I have always loved the hour just before the city fully wakes. The streets are quiet, but not empty, and the light has a softness that makes even ordinary buildings look briefly kind. A baker props open his door, and the smell of warm bread drifts out to meet the cool morning air. Somewhere above, a window opens, a kettle clicks, and a radio murmurs the news to nobody in particular. I like walking then because my thoughts seem to walk beside me instead of racing ahead or dragging behind. I think about small things, like whether the corner tree will bloom early this year, or whether I should finally learn to make soup properly. I think about larger things too, but gently, without demanding answers from them before breakfast. The city, half asleep, seems willing to let questions remain questions for a while. A bus sighs at the curb, an old man nods as if we share a secret, and a dog investigates every lamppost with serious devotion. By the time the sun clears the rooftops, the spell changes into ordinary hurry, phones out, shoulders up, everyone certain of somewhere to be. But I carry that earlier quiet with me through the day like a smooth stone in a pocket. When meetings multiply and messages pile like snow, I touch it in my mind and remember that slowness is not failure. It is simply the pace at which noticing happens. And noticing, I think, is where living actually begins.",
